            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Gerty Cori's Early Life</strong><br>\nGerty Cori was born in 1896 in Prague, which was then part of the Austro-Hungarian Empire. She was inspired by her father, a chemist, to pursue a career in science. Cori overcame gender barriers to study medicine at the German University of Prague, where she met her husband and collaborator, Carl Cori.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Cori Partnership</strong><br>\nGerty and Carl Cori worked closely throughout their careers, initially in Vienna and later in the United States. They researched carbohydrate metabolism, and their collaboration was a rare example of a husband-and-wife scientific team during a time when few women were acknowledged in science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Discovery of the Cori Cycle</strong><br>\nThe Coris discovered the process by which the human body metabolizes glycogen into glucose and reconverts it into glycogen for storage. This cycle, known as the Cori cycle, is fundamental to understanding cellular metabolism and energy conversion.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
