      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine</strong><br>\nGerty Cori, together with her husband, was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1947 for their discovery of the Cori cycle. She was the first woman to win the Nobel Prize in this category and only the third woman to win a Nobel Prize in science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Overcoming Obstacles</strong><br>\nDespite her significant contributions to biochemistry, Gerty Cori faced gender discrimination in her career. She often held research positions with little to no pay and was not initially acknowledged as a full collaborator with her husband by their peers or institutions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy in Medical Research</strong><br>\nGerty Cori's research extended beyond the Cori cycle to include studies on the enzyme phosphorylase and its role in glycogen breakdown. Her work laid the foundation for understanding diabetes and contributed to the development of treatments for metabolic diseases.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
